decisions:
  - id: three-tier-monitoring
    decision: 'Three monitoring tiers: free (Platform UI + watch), near-free (peek at $0.02/hr), full (poll only on demand)'
    rationale: 'User insight: polling doesn''t need AI. Separating detection (cheap) from processing (expensive) drops multi-agent coordination cost by 12x.'
  - id: peek-over-push
    decision: File-stat peek instead of true push/interrupt
    rationale: Claude Code doesn't support external interrupts. fs.stat every 10s is ~15 tokens — close enough to free that push architecture isn't worth the complexity.
  - id: status-blurb-on-heartbeat
    decision: Agent status blurbs piggyback on existing heartbeat mechanism
    rationale: Agents already call peek/poll regularly. Adding a status string costs nothing extra and gives humans immediate visibility into what each agent is doing.
learnings:
  - AI token cost is dominated by idle polling — separating detection from processing yields order-of-magnitude savings
  - File-system watching (fs.stat) is effectively free and sufficient for inter-process coordination on a single machine
  - The three-tier pattern (free human monitoring / near-free agent peek / full-cost agent processing) should be the default for any agent communication system
  - Multi-agent orchestration doesn't need complex infrastructure — JSONL files + file watching + a browser UI covers the full workflow
  - Status blurbs transform agents from opaque processes into observable musicians — small feature, massive UX impact
